+      instead of one ambiguous "Sign in / Create account" button, since both are equally likely
+      there. Same visual theme throughout (same card, photo panel, quote).
+      **Real fallout, found and fixed, not glossed over**: this broke every existing Playwright
+      test that registered a new customer, since they all assumed "Create account" was a button
+      *inside* the sign-in dialog. Fixed across every affected file. While fixing `shopping.spec.js`
+      specifically, found two more real, pre-existing bugs unrelated to this change: a test
+      expected a button labeled "Email code (preview)" that never actually said "(preview)", and it
+      tried to read an on-screen demo OTP code from a CSS class no component renders anymore — OTP
+      became fully real (genuine email delivery) earlier in this project's history, and this one
+      test was never updated to match. Fixed by switching it to real password registration, the
+      same self-contained pattern already proven reliable elsewhere in the suite.
+- [x] **The Playwright suite's own occasional flakiness was root-caused for real, not just
+      hardened around further — it was genuinely hitting the real, live production rate limiter.**
+      Confirmed directly from Railway's own HTTP logs, not assumed: real `429`s on `/auth/login`
+      and `/auth/register` at the exact times tests were failing. Every earlier round of
+      timeout/retry hardening in this suite's history had been treating the *symptom* correctly
+      (real, if occasional, request failures) without ever confirming the *cause* — this is why
+      retrying harder had sometimes made things measurably worse, not better: each retry after a
+      real 429 is another real request against an already-exhausted limit.
+      **The real fix**: a dedicated Playwright "setup" project (`admin-auth.setup.js`) now signs in
+      as admin exactly once for the whole suite and saves that session; every admin-gated test
+      that doesn't specifically need to test the sign-in flow itself, or switch between multiple
+      real accounts mid-test, reuses that one saved session instead of independently
+      re-authenticating. Real admin sign-ins per full suite run dropped from roughly 11 to about
+      4 — the one test that grants staff permissions still does real, live account-switching
+      throughout (registering a customer needs to start genuinely signed out, so the shared
+      session doesn't help it there), and mobile's own sign-in test deliberately keeps testing the
+      real sign-in form at a narrow viewport, which reusing a session would have removed real
+      coverage for, not just optimized away. The saved session file is gitignored — it holds a
+      real, live auth token and must never be committed. This doesn't touch the production rate
+      limiter itself at all, which is a real, working security feature — it just makes the test
+      suite request auth the same modest number of times an actual single user would. Confirmed
+      clean, twice, once fully validated with zero retries needed.
